def add(sub,b,title,why,facts,static,analysis,trap,mains,src='PRS',day=0,ap=False,p='P1',precision=None):
 J.append(dict(id=f'E15-2608-{len(J)+1:03}',date=f'2026-08-{day:02}' if day else '2026-08-31',date_precision=precision or ('day' if day else 'month'),subject=sub,block=b,title=title,why=why,facts=facts.split('|'),static_link=static,analysis=analysis.split('|'),trap=trap,mains=mains,priority=p,ap=ap,sources=['E15-'+x for x in src.split(',')],status='Historical August-indexed selected research; legal stages, release periods and thematic revision distinguished. Not latest-law, complete-month or implementation certification.',checked='2026-11-07',evidence_access='Registry records passages actually read. Analysis, static links and exam prompts are original guidance; primary instruments and implementation not all independently verified.'))
add('Economy','01 · Measurement and monetary policy','Q1 GDP: distinguish growth, prices and the data vintage','MoSPI released April–June FY2026–27 estimates on 31 August, using constant 2022–23 prices. The publication also describes revisions to the new national-accounts series.','Real GDP growth is estimated at 7.8%, compared with 10.3% nominal growth; real GVA grows 8.2%. These are year-on-year quarterly rates, not full-year outcomes.|Agriculture and allied activities grow 3.6%, manufacturing 9.2%, while mining contracts 2.4%. Sectoral expansion is therefore uneven despite the strong aggregate.|Real GFCF grows 11.9% and private final consumption 7.1%. The release gives real GDP ₹81.36 lakh crore and nominal GDP ₹88.27 lakh crore; use prose rather than conflicting chart approximations.','GDP at market prices = GVA at basic prices + product taxes − product subsidies. Constant-price estimates isolate volume changes from price effects.','Compare investment, consumption and sector composition before attributing growth to a single programme. GFCF growth is not automatically private corporate investment or proof that every announced project is operational.|For AP, connect construction, manufacturing, farm incomes and services through comparable state data; national quarterly growth cannot be assigned unchanged to the state. Preserve revisions and base-year labels in answer writing.','7.8% is Q1 real GDP growth, not August monthly growth, nominal growth or an RBI forecast.','How should investment-led growth be evaluated beyond the headline GDP rate?','GDP',31)
add('Economy','01 · Measurement and monetary policy','New-series IIP: a fourth sector matters','The 28 August primary release reports July quick estimates under the 2022–23 base and provides revised June figures. Its sectoral table corrects an incomplete three-sector shorthand in the secondary review.','July IIP grows 6.7% year-on-year. Manufacturing grows 7.3%, electricity and gas supply 8.7%, water supply/sewerage/waste management 7.4%; mining contracts 0.9%.|Sector weights are mining 11.053%, manufacturing 76.062%, electricity/gas 10.865% and water/sewerage/waste 2.020%: together exactly 100%.|April–July cumulative growth is 6.3%. The release mentions June’s earlier quick 7.3%, while the revised table gives 8.8%; the difference is a data-vintage issue, not two interchangeable final estimates.','IIP measures industrial production volumes, not GDP value addition. Sectoral and use-based classifications answer different questions.','Capital goods grow 16.1% and consumer non-durables contract 1.0% in July, illustrating why aggregate growth can coexist with weak segments. Do not infer household welfare solely from industrial output.|For exam tables, record reference month, release date, base, quick/final status and cumulative period. A series revision can alter weights, coverage and earlier rates; comparing old and new series requires methodological care.','July is the reference month; August is publication. The fourth sector is not a rounding error.','Explain why statistical rebasing changes both coverage and interpretation.','IIP',28)
add('Economy','01 · Measurement and monetary policy','August MPC: unchanged rates do not remove supply risks','RBI’s 5 August resolution records a unanimous decision to retain the repo rate and continue a neutral stance amidst energy-price and monsoon uncertainty.','Repo remains 5.25%, SDF 5.00%, and MSF/Bank Rate 5.50%. These are distinct administered policy rates, not a universal bank lending rate.|The resolution identifies West Asian conflict, volatile oil prices and deficient/uneven monsoon as risks to growth and inflation.|Its narrative distinguishes food/fuel pressures from core inflation. Some quarterly forecast values in prose and chart disagree; disputed forecasts are not used here as settled data.','The SDF–repo–MSF corridor supports liquidity management. Monetary-policy transmission operates through money markets, deposit/lending rates, expectations and demand.','A neutral stance means flexibility as conditions evolve, not a promise that the next move must be a cut. Supply shocks complicate the inflation–growth trade-off because tighter demand cannot directly produce rainfall or oil.|For AP agriculture and MSMEs, examine financing costs alongside food supply, irrigation and energy exposure. Distinguish projected inflation from subsequently observed CPI and policy rates from actual borrower rates.','Unchanged repo is neither a rate cut nor a guarantee that every bank leaves all rates unchanged.','Evaluate monetary-policy choices under simultaneous food and imported-energy shocks.','RBI',5)
add('Polity & Governance','01 · Parliament and institutional checks','Monsoon session: legislative output versus deliberation','PRS reviews the session held from 20 July to 13 August. The month is a useful case study in parliamentary productivity, scrutiny and the difference between legislative stages.','PRS records 12 Bills introduced and 11 passed; these totals are session-level, not a count of final Acts independently verified here.|Lok Sabha functioned for about 15% and Rajya Sabha 33% of scheduled time, according to the review.|The Indian Statistical Institute Bill remained pending; the foreign-contribution amendment was referred to a joint committee. A committee referral is not enactment or rejection.','Ordinary Bills require both Houses, followed by the constitutional assent process. Detailed committee scrutiny is an important deliberative mechanism.','Output counts cannot alone establish legislative quality: assess debate time, published evidence, committee consideration, fiscal implications and stakeholder participation. Low sitting time can coexist with many voice-vote passages.|Use institutional rather than partisan analysis: stronger scheduling, reasoned ministerial responses and post-legislative review can improve accountability. For APPSC, compare state legislative scrutiny without assuming identical bicameral proceedings.','Passage, presidential assent and commencement are separate stages; “passed” does not certify operative law.','Does counting Bills passed adequately measure parliamentary effectiveness?')
add('Polity & Governance','02 · Federalism and legislative proposals','MMDR amendment: mineral revenues and fiscal federalism','PRS reports parliamentary passage of the mines-and-minerals amendment. The reviewed proposal raises issues of central regulation, mineral-bearing land and state revenue powers.','The Bill addresses restrictions connected with levies on mineral-bearing land and the Union’s role in mineral regulation.|Mineral development, royalty and taxation must be analysed under their specific constitutional and statutory bases; a generic “all mining revenue belongs to one tier” claim is misleading.|The underlying final Act, assent and commencement were not independently inspected; this note describes the parliamentary-stage review, not a certified current consolidated law.','Union List Entry 54 and State List Entries 23 and 50 frame the interaction of mineral development and mineral-right taxation, subject to constitutional wording.','Evaluate predictable investment rules against the fiscal needs of mineral-bearing states and affected communities. Revenue rules do not by themselves ensure ecological compliance, rehabilitation or local benefit sharing.|For AP, relate the debate to mining administration and district-level accountability, while avoiding invented figures for state revenue loss. A defensible answer separates legal competence, revenue incidence and developmental outcomes.','A royalty, a tax and a regulatory fee are not interchangeable merely because all relate to minerals.','Discuss the balance between national mineral strategy and state fiscal autonomy.')
add('Economy','02 · Enterprise finance and regulation','MSME amendment: prompt payment needs enforcement','PRS reports passage of the MSME amendment, including threshold-related powers, dispute mediation and CPSE invoice handling on TReDS. Final commencement was not independently verified.','The reviewed changes provide for notified enterprise thresholds rather than treating every numerical classification limit as permanently fixed in the statute.|A 90-day mediation mechanism is part of the dispute-resolution design reviewed by PRS.|CPSE invoices on TReDS are addressed to strengthen receivables financing; invoice registration, buyer acceptance, financing and eventual payment remain different stages.','TReDS facilitates discounting of trade receivables through participating financiers. It is not the same as a government grant or cancellation of a buyer’s liability.','Liquidity problems are often caused by buyer power and delayed acceptance, not just lack of credit. Digital onboarding must be paired with credible invoice validation, timely decisions and affordable dispute settlement.|For AP’s small suppliers, assess access costs, bargaining asymmetry, awareness and buyer compliance. Track payment ageing and successful remedies rather than only platform registrations or sanctioned credit.','A statutory or proposed mediation timeline does not prove every dispute actually concludes within that time.','Can receivables platforms solve MSME payment delays without buyer accountability?')
add('Polity & Governance','03 · Corporate governance and accountability','Corporate-laws JPC: recommendations are not amendments in force','The August review covers joint-committee recommendations on corporate law, including CSR, auditors, NFRA and company-law institutions. They remain recommendations unless lawfully adopted.','The JPC report discussed changes affecting corporate social responsibility obligations and compliance design.|Auditor accountability and the role of the National Financial Reporting Authority are part of the institutional debate.|The report also considers NCLT-related functioning; a recommendation cannot be cited as if it automatically changes the Companies Act or tribunal jurisdiction.','Corporate governance links fiduciary duties, disclosure, independent audit and enforcement. CSR spending obligations and company solvency are separate legal questions.','A balanced answer asks whether reforms reduce duplicative compliance while preserving investor, creditor and public-interest safeguards. Independence without expertise is weak, but expertise without accountability can enable capture.|Strengthen reasoned regulatory orders, audit quality, transparent appointments and predictable adjudication. Measure compliance burden alongside fraud detection, timely insolvency decisions and confidence in financial reporting.','A committee report is evidence of proposed reform, not a notified exemption or binding new threshold.','How can corporate-law simplification preserve credible oversight?',p='P2')
add('Economy','03 · Industrial strategy and trade','BHAVYA–Rasayan: shared infrastructure for chemical parks','PRS records approval of support for three greenfield chemical parks under BHAVYA–Rasayan, with a multi-year central outlay and state participation.','The reviewed outlay is ₹3,030 crore for FY2026–27 to FY2030–31.|Central support is capped at ₹1,000 crore per park; state participation includes land and a minimum ₹500 crore contribution under the reviewed design.|The scheme concerns common infrastructure for greenfield parks, not proof that three completed industrial clusters have already begun production.','Industrial clustering can reduce logistics and utility costs through shared infrastructure, but chemical processes create hazardous-waste, water and emergency-management obligations.','Assess site suitability, environmental appraisal, common effluent treatment, emergency response and cumulative exposure before equating investment with sustainable growth. Subsidy ceilings are not guaranteed expenditure for every applicant.|For AP, this is a comparative industrial-policy opportunity, not a verified state allocation. Require transparent selection, credible utilities and local employment/supplier linkages; track commissioned facilities and compliance rather than MoUs alone.','Three approved parks nationally does not establish that any named AP district has been selected.','Evaluate the benefits and risks of cluster-based chemical industrialisation.',p='P2')
add('Economy','03 · Industrial strategy and trade','E-commerce exports: separate the exporter from the online seller','The reviewed framework distinguishes the Exporter of Record and Seller of Record to support small online exporters while allocating regulatory and payment responsibilities.','The framework uses separate entities for EOR and SOR and requires Indian-origin goods under the reviewed arrangement.|Confirmed export orders are distinguished from speculative inventory accumulation; payment to sellers is addressed through a seven-day requirement described by PRS.|Proportional rebate treatment and returned goods are part of the framework; exported returns are not simply ordinary domestic sales by default.','An exporter’s customs, foreign-exchange and tax responsibilities differ from a marketplace’s digital intermediation or an artisan’s production activity.','Small firms benefit when consolidated logistics and compliance services reduce fixed costs. However, unclear liability, valuation or payment disputes can transfer risk back to sellers; contracts and grievance channels matter.|For AP weavers and craft clusters, compare direct export, aggregator and EOR models on net realisation, return costs and market access. Do not assume that listing online eliminates product standards, customs compliance or buyer risk.','EOR and SOR are not automatically the same entity; an export facilitation framework is not a blanket tax waiver.','How can e-commerce exports become accessible to small producers without weakening compliance?',p='P2')
add('Economy','02 · Enterprise finance and regulation','Taxation amendment: study legal stage before financial effects','PRS reports that the Taxation Laws amendment was introduced on 4 August and subsequently passed. The reviewed legislative account is not a substitute for the final fiscal instrument.','The item belongs to the August parliamentary reform agenda; introduction and passage are distinct from commencement.|Tax amendments can alter liabilities or procedures only according to the relevant enacted text and effective-date clauses.|The exact final statutory changes and implementing notifications were not independently read, so this note does not invent rates, automatic refunds or AP-specific revenue estimates.','Direct taxes, indirect taxes and intergovernmental devolution operate through different constitutional and statutory arrangements. The title of a Bill does not establish its incidence.','In a Mains answer, evaluate certainty, simplicity, equity, compliance cost and administrability only after identifying the actual provision. A procedural amendment and a rate change can have very different distributional effects.|For revision, use a legal-stage ledger: proposal, parliamentary passage, assent, commencement and administrative instructions. This prevents a coaching headline from becoming an unsupported claim about current taxpayer obligations.','“Taxation Laws amendment passed” does not mean every taxpayer’s rate changed immediately.','Why are certainty and effective-date clarity central to tax governance?',p='P2')
add('Economy','02 · Enterprise finance and regulation','Bankers’ books: digital evidence still requires reliability','PRS records introduction on 3 August and passage of the Bankers’ Books evidence legislation. The theme links digital banking records to judicially usable evidence.','The reviewed legislation updates the treatment of bankers’ records in a digital environment.|Electronic records and certified copies remain subject to legal conditions; a screenshot is not automatically conclusive evidence of a debt or transaction.|Final enacted text, assent and commencement were not independently inspected in this batch; the note preserves the parliamentary-stage description.','Admissibility, authenticity, integrity, evidentiary weight and the opportunity to challenge a record are separate questions in the law of evidence.','Digitisation can reduce the burden of producing original records, but access logs, secure retention, audit trails and responsible certification remain essential. Cyber compromise and clerical error cannot be ruled out solely because data are electronic.|For APPSC analytical questions, connect efficient commercial adjudication to procedural fairness and financial inclusion. A person disputing an unauthorised transaction should have accessible evidence and meaningful redress, not an irrebuttable presumption based on a system entry.','Digital form does not make every banking entry accurate or immune to legal challenge.','How should evidence law balance digital efficiency with fairness?',p='P2')
add('Economy','02 · Enterprise finance and regulation','Foreign-asset disclosure: valuation dates are not filing dates','PRS reviews rules for foreign-asset disclosure, including a 31 December deadline and a 31 March valuation reference. Read these as separate compliance concepts.','The reviewed rules distinguish the reporting deadline from the date used to value relevant assets.|A disclosure framework concerns reporting obligations; it does not by itself prove that all disclosed holdings were legally acquired or fully tax-exempt.|The complete primary rules and person-specific eligibility conditions were not inspected; no personalised compliance or amnesty advice is supplied here.','Tax residence, asset location, beneficial ownership and disclosure obligations are distinct. A valuation reference date measures a stock, whereas income is generally a flow over time.','For examination purposes, explain how clear forms, information exchange and proportionate penalties improve compliance. Safeguards against arbitrary valuation and duplicative reporting are also important.|Avoid compressing a complex scheme into “declare and become immune”. Effective administration requires evidence of eligibility, consistent interpretation and review mechanisms; deadline extensions must be supported by actual notification.','31 March valuation and 31 December filing are not interchangeable; neither is automatically the acquisition date.','Assess the governance requirements of credible offshore-asset disclosure.',p='P2')
add('Science & Technology','01 · Energy transition and regulation','SHANTI draft rules: nuclear licensing is a staged process','PRS reviews draft rules and regulations under the nuclear reform framework, with comments invited until 4 September. This is consultation-stage material, not a final operating licence.','The drafts address composite licensing alongside phase-specific authorisations by the Atomic Energy Regulatory Board.|The reviewed design includes periodic consideration of liability limits, described on a five-year cycle.|A draft rule, a licence, a safety authorisation and a commercially operating nuclear facility are distinct stages; final instruments were not independently read here.','Nuclear governance must distinguish policy promotion, operator responsibility, independent safety oversight, liability and safeguards. Delegated legislation must stay within the parent statute.','Streamlined licensing can reduce uncertainty without collapsing safety gates. Regulatory capacity, transparent standards, emergency preparedness and credible enforcement are essential as participation broadens.|For AP, nuclear-policy relevance does not establish a new project approval, land award or construction date. Discuss technology, liability, local consultation and long-duration waste obligations as connected governance issues.','Comments invited on a draft do not mean the proposed rules are already operative.','Can single-window nuclear licensing coexist with independent phase-wise safety oversight?')
add('Economy','04 · Clean energy and circular economy','Unified GOBARdhan: bankability across the biogas value chain','The 6 August Cabinet release approves a national circular-bioenergy scheme administered by the Petroleum and Natural Gas Ministry. Its design combines demand, price, investment and infrastructure support.','Approved outlay is ₹23,731 crore for FY2026–27 to FY2035–36; nearly ten-fold production growth is a target, not a realised result.|The release specifies an administered CBG price of ₹2,110 per MMBTU and capital assistance up to ₹2 crore per tonne per day of installed eligible capacity.|Six components cover assured offtake, stable pricing, capital assistance, pipeline infrastructure, credit guarantee and an ecosystem challenge fund. Eligible brownfield capacity expansion is also addressed.','Anaerobic digestion produces biogas; upgrading removes impurities and raises methane concentration for CBG. Capacity in tonnes/day differs from an energy-price unit such as MMBTU.','Long-term viability depends on predictable feedstock, methane leakage control, digestate quality, water use and reliable gas evacuation. Capital subsidy alone cannot compensate for poorly designed local supply chains.|For AP, map cattle dung, crop residues and municipal organic waste without double-counting competing uses. Evaluate actual offtake, lifecycle emissions and farmer payments rather than only plant announcements.','₹2 crore/TPD is a capacity-linked assistance ceiling, not ₹2 crore per tonne of daily fuel sold.','Evaluate the institutional conditions for a sustainable waste-to-CBG economy.','GOBAR',6)
add('Economy','04 · Clean energy and circular economy','Domestic PNG incentive: reward active connections, not idle pipes','The 18 August approval introduces a company-level incentive effective from 1 September. It aims to convert dormant connections into billed use and expand domestic piped-gas access.','Eligible CGD entities receive an additional 200 SCM of lower-priced domestic gas for each incremental billed domestic connection above a geographical-area threshold.|The scheme operates in two tranches spanning six months; the allocation substitutes costlier LNG in the company’s CNG transport sourcing mix.|The release estimates a shorter connection-investment payback period, but this is an expected financial effect, not an audited realised return or guaranteed household tariff reduction.','PNG is piped natural gas; CNG is compressed natural gas. SCM is a standard-volume unit, unlike rupees, kilowatts or a household cash transfer.','Billed-connection incentives better align with usage than counting pipes alone. Yet household affordability, safe installation, metering, reliable supply and grievance redress determine durable adoption.|For AP urban areas, examine right-of-way coordination, network economics and customer choice. Promotional claims that gas is always cheaper or risk-free should not replace local tariff and safety analysis; lower emissions than some fuels do not make fossil gas zero-carbon.','200 SCM is an allocation to an eligible company, not a free-gas entitlement for every household.','How can clean-cooking policy distinguish infrastructure access from sustained affordable use?','PNG',18)
add('Polity & Governance','04 · Justice institutions','Tribunals reform: independence must accompany expertise','PRS records introduction on 10 August and parliamentary passage of the tribunals reform Bill. The proposed National Tribunals Commission is central to the institutional design.','The reviewed NTC model combines judicial and technical participation and addresses appointments and service conditions.|PRS describes a three-month appointment requirement; a statutory target is not evidence that every vacancy has been filled.|The final Act, assent and commencement were not independently established; the note does not assume a proposed institution is already functioning.','Articles 323A and 323B, separation of powers and constitutional judicial review frame tribunal design. Tribunals cannot eliminate the supervisory role of constitutional courts.','A government department can be both a major litigant and an influential administrator, creating conflicts of interest. Appointment design, tenure security, infrastructure and reasoned decision-making must be evaluated together.|For UPSC, compare expertise and accessible justice with risks of executive dominance and vacancy-driven delay. For APPSC, distinguish central tribunal reform from the jurisdiction and functioning of state forums.','Creating a commission on paper does not by itself establish judicial independence or reduce pendency.','What safeguards make specialist tribunals credible alternatives for first-instance adjudication?')
add('Polity & Governance','05 · Education and knowledge institutions','Indian Statistical Institute Bill: autonomy under a new structure','The ISI Bill was introduced on 3 August and remained pending in the reviewed session. Its institutional provisions therefore should not be taught as completed statutory transformation.','The reviewed proposal gives the institute a body-corporate, not-for-profit framework.|The President’s Visitor role and a Board-based governance structure are part of the Bill’s design.|Pending legislation does not itself replace the existing legal framework or prove that all proposed appointments and functions are operational.','Statistical capacity is public infrastructure: independent research, reproducible methods and skilled personnel support credible policy measurement. Institutional autonomy and public accountability are complementary.','Evaluate appointment processes, academic freedom, funding predictability and transparency rather than equating corporate form with privatisation. An institute can remain not-for-profit while acquiring a different legal structure.|Link the debate to GDP/IIP rebasing and trust in official evidence, without implying that one institute alone compiles every national series. Preserve the difference between government statistics production and broader statistical research and training.','A body corporate is not necessarily a profit-making private company; the Bill was pending in this review.','How can knowledge institutions balance autonomy, public funding and accountability?',day=3,p='P2')
add('Agriculture','01 · Cooperative institutions and farm inputs','NCDC amendment: finance is only one part of cooperative reform','PRS reports introduction on 10 August and passage of an amendment broadening NCDC’s financing framework. Final commencement was not independently checked.','The reviewed legislation expands the financing role of the National Cooperative Development Corporation.|A broader lending mandate is distinct from an automatic grant or loan waiver for every cooperative.|Financial assistance still needs viable projects, governance capacity and repayment arrangements; this review does not certify a specific AP cooperative’s sanction.','Cooperatives combine member ownership and democratic governance. Credit institutions, registrars, professional management and market linkages have different roles.','Cheaper or wider finance can fail if boards are captured, accounts unreliable or business models uncompetitive. Pair access with audited statements, member participation, professional skills and transparent procurement.|For AP dairy, fisheries and producer cooperatives, compare working-capital needs, value addition and market risk. Evaluate member income and institutional sustainability, not only aggregate loan approvals; political direction is not a substitute for fiduciary discipline.','NCDC finance is not synonymous with a universal budget grant or guaranteed profitable cooperative activity.','Why must cooperative finance reform be accompanied by governance reform?',p='P2')
add('Security','01 · Defence industrial capability','Missile technology transfer: approval is not production','On 25 August, the Defence Minister approved transfer of DRDO-developed technologies of conventional missile systems to Indian industry for domestic production. The primary release expressly retains qualifications and regulatory conditions.','The decision aims to move developed systems into industrial production and deepen the domestic defence manufacturing base.|Industry participation remains subject to applicable qualifications, certifications and regulatory requirements.|The release identifies opportunities for MSMEs and technology partners; it does not certify that every missile system has already been manufactured by private firms or cleared for export.','Technology transfer includes know-how and production capability, but technology absorption requires skilled people, tooling, testing, quality systems and reliable supply chains.','Strategic autonomy depends on reproducible production and maintenance, not only receipt of drawings. Protect sensitive knowledge while encouraging competition, domestic value addition and accountable procurement.|For AP industrial ecosystems, discuss supplier and skills opportunities without inventing a local factory award. Test outcomes through qualification milestones, delivery quality and operational reliability; a political approval is only one step in a long capability chain.','Conventional missile ToT approval is not an unconditional export licence or proof of mass production.','How can defence technology transfer produce sustained industrial capability?','TOT',25)
add('Security','01 · Defence industrial capability','Sixth positive indigenisation list: a phased demand signal','PRS reviews a sixth positive indigenisation list covering 405 items and an estimated import-substitution opportunity. Item-specific schedules matter more than the headline count.','The reviewed list contains 16 Coast Guard items and 389 items linked to defence public-sector undertakings.|PRS reports a potential domestic opportunity of about ₹3,070 crore; potential value is not an audited saving already achieved.|Indigenisation deadlines vary by item. The list should not be simplified into an immediate blanket ban on every listed import from the announcement date.','Demand assurance can support domestic supplier investment, but qualification, standards, intellectual property and supply security determine effective substitution.','Analyse whether suppliers gain design and lifecycle capability or merely replace the final assembly stage. Time-bound procurement predictability must be balanced with military readiness and acceptable quality.|For APPSC, link MSME participation to testing access, payment reliability and certification support. Avoid calling all procurement expenditure domestic value addition, since imported subsystems can remain significant.','405 items does not mean 405 completed indigenous products or an identical import cutoff for all.','Assess positive indigenisation lists as an industrial-policy tool.',p='P2')
add('Polity & Governance','06 · Digital identity and citizen safeguards','Telecom user identification: inclusion must survive stronger KYC','The Gazette notifies Telecommunications User Identification Rules on 21 August, with publication-date commencement. The opening primary passage confirms status; detailed later provisions are followed through PRS.','The framework uses biometric identification and distinguishes Aadhaar-based e-KYC from digital KYC alternatives under specified circumstances.|The reviewed system addresses live capture, business-user representatives and identification responsibilities rather than only a customer’s name.|PRS also describes limited transfers to relatives or legal heirs; detailed eligibility should be checked in the full operative text before individual advice.','Identity assurance, authentication, subscriber registration and citizenship are different concepts. Biometrics are sensitive personal information, not an infallible proof of intent or innocence.','Better verification can reduce impersonation while excluding people with biometric failure, disability or documentation gaps if alternatives are weak. Evaluate assisted access, secure retention, correction channels and effective redress alongside fraud reduction.|For AP’s rural and tribal users, reliable connectivity and trained points of sale are essential. Do not infer that all existing residents must immediately lose service or that Aadhaar is the only permissible route in every case.','Primary commencement is verified; every detailed KYC exception was not directly read from later Gazette pages.','How can digital KYC strengthen security without creating exclusion?','TELECOM,PRS',21)
add('Society & Welfare','01 · Public health regulation','Drug-rule debarment: enforce quality with due process','PRS reviews amended drug rules addressing misleading submissions and debarment. The policy issue is trustworthy regulation rather than automatic denial of every future application.','The reviewed rules respond to false or misleading documents in regulatory submissions.|Show-cause procedures and an appeal mechanism form part of the described framework.|The complete primary notification and every sanction condition were not inspected; the note does not invent a universal debarment duration or claim all rejected applicants committed criminal offences.','Licensing, administrative sanctions, civil liability and criminal prosecution are distinct regulatory tools. Natural justice requires notice and a meaningful opportunity to respond.','Effective deterrence needs competent inspections, reliable laboratories and interoperable records. Overbroad sanctions can reduce legitimate supply, while weak enforcement undermines patient safety and honest competition.|For AP pharmaceutical and healthcare settings, propose risk-based inspections, traceable samples and reasoned decisions. A regulator’s power should be judged by evidence quality and public-health outcomes as well as the number of penalties.','An administrative debarment process is not automatically a criminal conviction.','Explain how medicines regulation can combine deterrence and procedural fairness.',p='P2')
add('Society & Welfare','01 · Public health regulation','Healthcare committee reports: recommendations are not entitlements','The August review includes parliamentary recommendations on healthcare access, pharmaceutical regulation, chronic kidney disease and vector-borne disease control. A proposed benefit expansion is not an operative patient entitlement.','Committee concerns include public-health capacity, affordable medicines and the effectiveness of institutional oversight.|The reviewed suggestion of higher health coverage, including a ₹10 lakh figure, is a recommendation rather than proof of automatic nationwide implementation.|Disease-control and pricing recommendations require budgetary, administrative or legal follow-through; the full primary reports were not independently read.','Financial protection, service availability, clinical quality and prevention are separate dimensions of universal health coverage. Insurance cannot substitute for all public-health functions.','For AP, CKD prevention and continuity of care require diagnostics, water-quality investigation, specialist referral and sustainable dialysis access. Avoid attributing every local disease cluster to one unverified cause.|Monitor actual benefits, claim denials, treatment quality and out-of-pocket expenditure rather than only announced coverage ceilings. Public-health surveillance and vector control need local coordination beyond hospital financing.','A committee’s ₹10 lakh recommendation is not a new insurance card or automatic cash payment.','Why must universal-health policy integrate prevention, services and financial protection?')
add('Society & Welfare','02 · Rural employment and social accountability','Rural-employment audit draft: financial and social scrutiny differ','The 7 August Gazette invites comments on draft audit rules under VB–G RAM G. The draft’s own proposed commencement wording must not be mistaken for a final operative notification.','Annual financial audit and a social audit in every Gram Panchayat at least once in six months are in the inspected opening provisions.|The draft provides for an independent social-audit unit facilitating Gram Sabha scrutiny; financial records and community verification serve complementary purposes.|PRS describes proposed seven-day publication and 30-day action-taken reporting, with later recovery/closure timelines. Those detailed later clauses were not directly inspected in this batch.','Article 243A, local participation and CAG-related financial oversight illuminate different accountability mechanisms. Social audit is not merely an internal departmental inspection.','A public hearing without follow-up can expose irregularities yet leave workers unpaid. Strengthen independent facilitation, accessible records, protected testimony, reasoned action and appeals, while preventing unsafe disclosure of personal data.|For AP, connect wage-payment records to worker receipts and actual assets across the employment-law transition. Digital dashboards cannot replace independent verification or presume that every discrepancy is proven fraud.','This is a draft consultation, not proof that all proposed deadlines are already binding final rules.','What makes social audit effective beyond the conduct of Gram Sabha meetings?','AUDIT,PRS',7)
add('Polity & Governance','07 · Local self-government','Panchayati Raj review: devolution needs staff and fiscal space','PRS reviews parliamentary recommendations on the implementation of the 73rd Amendment and rural-development capacity. Constitutional recognition alone does not prove effective decentralisation.','The committee discussion addresses the functioning of local institutions and the resources required to perform devolved responsibilities.|Rural skills and administrative capability are relevant to local economic development and service delivery.|These are committee recommendations, not independently verified new transfers of all functions, funds and functionaries to every Panchayat.','Part IX, the Eleventh Schedule, Gram Sabha participation and State Finance Commissions provide a framework; actual devolution depends on state laws and administrative arrangements.','Local bodies need predictable revenue, trained staff and clear responsibility, not only additional tasks. A scheme delivered within a village is not necessarily controlled by its elected Panchayat.|For AP, examine own-source revenue, tied versus untied grants, staff accountability and participation by women and marginalised residents. Judge decentralisation through local discretion and service outcomes, while preserving safeguards against elite capture.','The Eleventh Schedule does not automatically transfer every listed function without state-level legal and administrative action.','Assess the gap between constitutional decentralisation and effective local self-government.',p='P2')
add('Polity & Governance','07 · Local self-government','Digital land records: mapping is not conclusive title','PRS reviews recommendations concerning DILRMP and SVAMITVA. Land-information modernisation links survey technology, record correction and legally meaningful dispute resolution.','DILRMP concerns modernisation and integration of land records; SVAMITVA focuses on surveying rural inhabited areas and associated property records.|Survey completion, issue of a property document and resolution of competing claims are different administrative milestones.|Committee recommendations in the review do not establish a universal new conclusive-title law or an automatic right to bank credit.','A cadastral map represents parcel boundaries; a record of rights, mutation and registered transaction have related but distinct legal functions.','Digitising an erroneous record can make exclusion more durable unless public notice, assisted correction and appeals are effective. Protect common lands, customary claims and women’s rights during verification.|For AP, pair geospatial accuracy with revenue and registration coordination, accessible dispute resolution and clear legal status. Assess actual service improvement and credit usability, not only numbers of drones, maps or uploaded files.','A drone survey or mutation entry is not automatically an indefeasible judicial determination of ownership.','How can land-record digitisation strengthen tenure security without reproducing exclusion?',p='P2')
add('Economy','05 · Infrastructure risk allocation','Road concession model: allocate traffic risk explicitly','PRS reviews changes to the road model concession agreement. The issue is how BOT projects distribute demand, capacity, finance and dispute risk between public authority and concessionaire.','BOT conventionally means Build–Operate–Transfer; an inconsistent expansion in the secondary text is not copied here.|The reviewed changes address substantial traffic shortfalls during early target dates and mandatory buyback in specified sustained over-capacity circumstances.|Exact dispute-value boundaries and every contractual trigger were not independently verified from the primary model; this note deliberately withholds an uncertain arbitration cutoff.','A concession agreement assigns construction, operating, financing and demand risks. Public–private partnership does not eliminate contingent public liabilities.','Risk should rest with the party best able to manage it. Traffic guarantees can improve bankability but create fiscal exposure, especially when forecasting is optimistic or renegotiation becomes routine.|For AP roads, test demand assumptions, alternative routes, toll affordability and public-disclosure quality. Compare contract terms and realised traffic rather than treating one national model summary as a signed state concession.','BOT transfers the asset under contractual terms; it is not permanent private ownership or a risk-free public project.','Evaluate traffic-risk sharing in road public–private partnerships.',p='P2')
add('Environment','01 · Disaster resilience','Disaster-management recommendations: shift from response to risk','PRS reviews committee recommendations on disaster management, including drainage, resilient infrastructure, building codes and river-basin flood planning. These are proposed improvements, not a completed national risk reduction programme.','The committee emphasises stronger drainage and enforcement of building standards as preventive measures.|River-basin management includes flood mapping, protection of floodplains and restoration of natural drainage.|Institutional recommendations must be distinguished from actual hazard-specific alerts, district plans and verified spending; the full primary report was not independently inspected.','Hazard, exposure, vulnerability and capacity jointly shape disaster risk. Flood control structures do not remove all residual risk, especially downstream or beyond design assumptions.','Urban flooding often reflects land-use and maintenance failures as much as extreme rainfall. Improve drainage inventories, basin coordination, evacuation planning and safe infrastructure siting, not only emergency compensation.|For coastal and riverine AP, integrate cyclone shelters, reservoir operations, district warnings and vulnerable-population access. Avoid interpreting every intense flood as a cloudburst or every announced work as a functioning protection asset.','A committee recommendation is neither a new disaster declaration nor proof that every floodplain has been mapped.','Why should disaster governance prioritise exposure and vulnerability alongside forecasting?')
add('Society & Welfare','03 · Inclusion and development finance','NSFDC review: credit access requires functioning delivery agencies','PRS reviews the committee report on the National Scheduled Castes Finance and Development Corporation, highlighting institutional vacancies, equity and state-channel delivery.','The committee identifies irregular equity infusion, vacancies and under-performance of State Channelising Agencies.|Recommendations include stronger delivery institutions, easier access, regular equity support and integration with skill-development programmes.|A proposed increase in the loan-eligibility income limit is not an independently verified operative new threshold or automatic loan approval.','Development finance differs from a universal transfer. State Channelising Agencies implement assistance mechanisms, while eligibility, project viability and repayment remain important.','A fund can exist without reaching excluded applicants. Assisted applications, transparent selection, timely disbursement, mentoring and grievance channels help convert financial provision into capability.|For AP’s eligible SC entrepreneurs, evaluate geography, collateral barriers, market access and institutional follow-through. Success should include viable enterprises and borrower income, not merely amounts sanctioned or applications collected.','NSFDC assistance is not identical to every SC scholarship, reservation benefit or debt-waiver scheme.','How can targeted development finance reduce exclusion rather than add administrative barriers?',p='P2')
add('International Relations','01 · Central Asia and connectivity','India–Uzbekistan: partnership ambitions need connectivity','PRS records the Prime Minister’s 29–30 August visit, elevation to a comprehensive strategic partnership and a bilateral trade target. The attempted MEA outcomes link returned only a navigation shell.','The reviewed bilateral trade target is US$5 billion by 2030, not already achieved annual trade.|Cooperation areas include geology/minerals, tourism, environment, higher education, scientific research, Indian systems of medicine and culture.|An Economic and Financial Dialogue between the finance ministries is among the reviewed outcomes. These specifics rely on read PRS, not a substantive MEA body independently accessed here.','Uzbekistan is doubly landlocked in Central Asia. Connectivity constraints, regional institutions and diversified transport corridors shape India’s engagement.','Strategic partnership titles should be assessed against transport feasibility, commercial demand, investment protection and actual implementation. Geography can constrain trade even when political intent is strong.|For a map answer, locate neighbouring Central Asian states and distinguish bilateral cooperation from membership of regional organisations. India’s mineral and educational interests can complement cultural links, but no target is guaranteed by an MoU.','US$5 billion is a future target, not a realised trade total or grant commitment.','Assess the opportunities and connectivity constraints in India–Central Asia relations.',day=30,p='P2')
add('Society & Welfare','04 · Urban basic services','AMRUT sewerage review: connections and safe cleaning matter','The parliamentary review summarised by PRS identifies slow sewerage/septage progress, inadequate treatment and continued hazardous manual practices. Recommendations are not evidence of completed urban-service coverage.','The committee seeks stricter timelines, milestone-based monitoring and prioritised household sewer connections.|It recommends septic-tank standards, additional sewage/faecal-sludge treatment capacity, resource recovery and treated-water reuse networks.|Mechanised sewer cleaning and sanitation-worker wages, health insurance and social security form an explicit labour-protection dimension.','Sewerage networks, septage management and wastewater treatment are not interchangeable. A treatment plant without conveyance and household links can remain underused.','Track the full service chain: containment, collection, transport, treatment, safe disposal or reuse. Engineering assets must be paired with operations funding, water-quality monitoring and worker safety.|For AP urban local bodies, link AMRUT investments to safe mechanisation, contractor accountability and affordable connections. A city-level project sanction does not prove every household is served or that dangerous entry into sewers has ended.','Installed treatment capacity is not the same as actual sewage safely treated or a connected household count.','How can urban sanitation programmes integrate infrastructure performance and worker dignity?')
add('Environment','02 · Forests and landscape governance','Himalayan forest fires: recommendations for continuous vigilance','PRS reviews the committee report on Himalayan forest fires, covering climate-linked risk, response capacity, regeneration and funding. A recommendation to notify a disaster is not itself a disaster notification.','The committee proposes region-specific climate–fire studies and predictive risk models.|Recommendations include round-the-clock satellite observation, better interpretation of alerts and training of nodal officers.|Post-fire watershed/spring rejuvenation and Oak/Deodar regeneration missions are proposed; additional response teams and formal disaster recognition require follow-through.','Fire risk depends on fuel, weather, terrain and ignition. A satellite alert indicates a thermal anomaly or potential fire; it does not alone measure ecological damage or identify culpability.','Combine remote sensing with local verification, prevention, accessible reporting and rapid response. Regeneration should consider native ecology, soils and water systems rather than treating every plantation as equivalent recovery.|For AP, use this as comparative landscape-governance learning, not a claim that Himalayan species-restoration proposals apply unchanged to Eastern Ghats forests. Track response time and ecological recovery, not only alerts counted.','A proposal for a dedicated satellite or disaster status is not proof that it has been implemented.','What should a science-based forest-fire governance system include?',p='P2')
add('Society & Welfare','04 · Urban basic services','Non-revenue water: not every loss is a leaking pipe','Selected August magazine passages frame urban water security through non-revenue water. This is thematic consolidation, not a verified new August water-loss census or AP utility audit.','NRW is the difference between water supplied to a network and water generating billed revenue.|Components include real physical losses, apparent/commercial losses and authorised unbilled consumption; not all non-revenue water disappears before reaching a user.|Leakage, faulty metering, unauthorised use and billing gaps require different remedies. Magazine-wide city percentages are not independently reproduced as current audited estimates.','Urban utility performance links water balance, distribution pressure, metering, operations finance and the right to accessible basic services.','District-metered areas, GIS asset registers and calibrated meters help identify causes before expensive network expansion. Repair and pressure management may save both water and pumping energy, but a universal numerical target cannot replace a local baseline.|For AP towns, pair technically sound water balances with lifeline affordability, grievance redress and fair regularisation. Revenue improvement should not punish poor users for utility errors or justify indiscriminate disconnection.','NRW is not identical to physical leakage: some water reaches users legally but remains unbilled.','Can reducing non-revenue water improve both municipal finance and equity?','DRISHTI',p='P2')
add('Economy','06 · Services competitiveness','Professional services: regulation can protect quality or restrict entry','Selected magazine passages discuss NITI Aayog’s review of professional-service regulation. The original report was not independently inspected, so precise rankings and comparative restriction scores are withheld.','The reviewed themes include overlapping professional registration, licensing requirements and barriers to cross-border service provision.|Continuous professional development, transparent qualification criteria, appeals and mutual recognition are proposed directions rather than universal implemented reforms.|GATS distinguishes cross-border supply, consumption abroad, commercial presence and the presence of natural persons as four different modes.','Professional standards address information asymmetry and safety, but can also create exclusion or regulatory capture. International recognition differs from automatic migration rights.','Evaluate whether rules are necessary, proportionate and consistently administered. Digital processing and published timelines can reduce arbitrary delays without removing legitimate qualification checks.|For AP’s skilled workforce and service enterprises, connect education quality, mobility and access to high-value markets. An export target or agreement does not eliminate domestic professional licensing or overseas immigration conditions.','GATS Mode 4 concerns natural persons supplying services; it is not the same as unrestricted permanent migration.','How can professional regulation support quality and competitiveness together?','DRISHTI',p='P2')
add('Economy','04 · Clean energy and circular economy','Greenium: lower yield is not guaranteed environmental impact','The August compilation discusses sovereign green bonds and greenium. This note retains the financial concept, not an independently verified completed H1 FY2026–27 market average as of August.','Greenium describes a lower yield accepted on a green bond relative to an otherwise comparable conventional bond.|For comparable cash flows, a lower yield corresponds to a higher bond price; the comparison must account for maturity, liquidity and market conditions.|Green-use proceeds require credible allocation and reporting. A label or price premium does not by itself prove additional emissions reductions.','Bond prices and yields move inversely. Use-of-proceeds green bonds are distinct from instruments whose payment terms depend on sustainability performance.','Demand from mandated or sustainability-focused investors may lower borrowing costs, but weak disclosure can create greenwashing. Evaluate project eligibility, independent review, allocation timing and measured outcomes.|For AP green investment, avoid assuming that a sovereign greenium transfers automatically to every municipal or corporate issuer. Credit risk, liquidity and credible project pipelines remain important.','A positive greenium generally means lower yield, not that the green bond necessarily pays higher interest.','What safeguards make green finance additional and credible?','DRISHTI',p='P2')
add('Environment','03 · Cryosphere and physical geography','Zanskar glaciers: slower movement can accompany warming','Selected magazine passages discuss a study of 12 Zanskar Basin glaciers over 1992–2023. The original paper was not independently inspected; exact trend values and a projected peak-water year are not asserted as universal forecasts.','The reviewed mechanism links thinning to reduced driving stress and slower glacier movement; slower flow does not necessarily imply glacier recovery.|Glacier response varies with geometry, debris cover and terminus conditions, making basin-level generalisation hazardous.|Zanskar lies in Ladakh; the Zanskar River joins the Indus at Nimmu. Glacier storage links upstream cryosphere change to downstream seasonal water availability.','Glacier mass balance, flow velocity and runoff are different variables. Peak water describes an initial rise followed by decline in melt contribution as stored ice is depleted.','Strengthen ice-thickness, mass-balance and weather observations alongside basin water planning. Consider snow, groundwater and changing demand rather than equating every river-flow change with glacier melt alone.|For AP, the transferable lesson is climate-sensitive basin management, not direct dependence on Zanskar melt. Distinguish Himalayan hydrology from peninsular rainfall-fed river systems in geography answers.','A glacier that moves more slowly may be thinning; velocity alone is not a complete health indicator.','Explain why glacier-flow trends must be interpreted alongside mass balance and basin hydrology.','DRISHTI',p='P2')
add('Environment','01 · Disaster resilience','Cloudburst versus prolonged heavy rain: use the timescale','The August magazine discusses IMD’s distinction between prolonged heavy rainfall and a cloudburst in reporting on Upper Assam floods. This is selected secondary review, not a direct rainfall-station dataset inspection.','A cloudburst is generally described by IMD as rainfall of at least 100 mm in an hour over a small area, often around 20–30 square kilometres.|Intense rain can cause flash floods or landslides, but a flood alone does not establish that the hourly cloudburst threshold was met.|Orographic lifting and strong convective development can concentrate rainfall; prolonged regional rain has a different spatial and temporal pattern.','Rainfall intensity, accumulated rainfall, catchment saturation, runoff and flood discharge are related but distinct measurements.','Accurate classification matters for warning systems and accountability. Poor drainage, floodplain construction and unstable slopes can convert non-cloudburst rainfall into severe disaster; a dramatic label should not obscure manageable exposure.|For AP, combine IMD warnings with catchment observations, drainage maintenance and evacuation thresholds. Require time-stamped local evidence before attributing a damaging event to a cloudburst.','100 mm in a day is not the same as 100 mm in an hour; not every flood is a cloudburst.','Why does precise hazard classification matter for disaster-risk reduction?','DRISHTI',p='P2')
add('Environment','02 · Forests and landscape governance','Western Ghats ESA: draft boundaries need evidence and consultation','The August compilation discusses a reissued Western Ghats ecologically sensitive area draft. The exact primary draft number/date was not independently verified, so this is thematic revision rather than a claimed final August notification.','The proposed landscape spans Gujarat, Maharashtra, Goa, Karnataka, Kerala and Tamil Nadu; Andhra Pradesh is not one of these six Western Ghats states.|The reviewed approach differentiates restrictive treatment of mining/high-pollution activity from continued local livelihood uses and regulated development.|Gadgil and Kasturirangan committee approaches differ in coverage and zoning; neither committee recommendation alone constitutes a final operative notification.','An ESA is a landscape regulatory approach under environmental law; it should not be casually equated with every protected-area Eco-Sensitive Zone or a national park.','Boundary accuracy, local consultation, compensation/incentives and enforcement capacity determine legitimacy. Satellite mapping needs ground verification, while blanket claims that all agriculture must stop can misrepresent the proposal.|For APPSC, compare Eastern Ghats conservation needs without copying the Western Ghats draft as an AP order. Preserve proposal status and assess cumulative impacts, not only individual project clearances.','A draft ESA is not a final notified ban everywhere in the Western Ghats, and it is not an AP-specific scheme.','How can ecological zoning reconcile landscape protection and livelihood security?','DRISHTI',p='P2')
add('Agriculture','02 · Soil health and sustainable production','Soil management: digital maps must change field practice','Selected August magazine passages review soil conservation, soil testing, natural farming and digital mapping. Scheme achievements and carbon-credit eligibility were not independently audited in this batch.','Soil Health Cards provide nutrient-status information and crop-specific recommendations; testing is useful only when samples and advice are reliable.|Soil organic matter influences water retention, structure and nutrient cycling, linking land management to resilience as well as productivity.|Natural-farming practices, balanced fertilisation and soil-resource mapping are policy tools with different mechanisms; none makes all external inputs universally unnecessary.','Alluvial, black, red and other soils differ in texture, mineral composition, drainage and crop suitability. Soil health is not captured by one nutrient or carbon number.','Combine representative testing, extension support and local trials with farmer cost/risk assessment. Carbon payments require credible baselines, additionality, measurement and verification; every farmer does not automatically receive a tradable credit.|For AP’s diverse agro-climatic zones, tailor nutrient and water management to crop and soil conditions. Digital platforms should support agronomy and accessible advice rather than substitute generic prescriptions for field evidence.','A soil map is not itself a fertiliser recommendation for every plot, and carbon storage is not automatically a saleable credit.','How can soil-information systems translate into resilient farm incomes?','DRISHTI',p='P2')
add('Science & Technology','02 · Biotechnology and responsible innovation','Generative biology: design capability creates dual-use risk','The August compilation discusses AI-assisted design of functional bacteriophage genomes. Exact trial counts and an August discovery date are not independently confirmed; the note is thematic scientific consolidation.','Generative models can learn sequence patterns and propose DNA, RNA or protein designs for experimental testing.|Bacteriophages infect bacteria; useful research directions include antimicrobial-resistance treatment, delivery systems and molecular discovery.|Computational design is not equivalent to a viable organism, safe therapy or approved clinical treatment; synthesis, testing and biosafety evaluation remain necessary.','Sequencing reads biological information; synthesis writes it; gene editing modifies it. AI design adds a proposal-generation capability rather than eliminating laboratory validation.','Dual-use risks arise when beneficial design tools amplify harmful capabilities. Match model access, sequence screening, laboratory oversight and responsible disclosure to demonstrable risk while preserving legitimate research.|For India and AP’s research/health ecosystem, invest in secure datasets, skilled reviewers and credible biosafety institutions. Avoid both “AI instantly creates any pathogen” alarmism and the claim that an open model is automatically harmless.','A viable bacteriophage in an experiment is not an approved human therapy or proof that every AI-designed genome works.','How should AI-enabled biological design be governed without stifling beneficial innovation?','DRISHTI')
add('Science & Technology','03 · Space capability and astronomy','Space-sector ecosystem: distinguish authorisation and mission success','Selected August magazine passages consolidate private participation, IN-SPACe, NSIL and future mission plans. Changing startup counts and future launch dates are not treated as independently verified completed outcomes.','IN-SPACe has an authorisation/facilitation role, NSIL undertakes commercial functions, and ISRO retains research and mission capabilities; these institutions are not interchangeable.|The 2023 space-policy framework and earlier opening to non-government participation support a wider ecosystem beyond direct government manufacturing.|Human spaceflight, station modules, docking and planetary missions involve staged development; planned launch windows do not prove flights occurred.','A space economy includes launch, spacecraft, ground infrastructure and downstream services. Revenue estimates, investment commitments and realised commercial output are distinct.','Evaluate safety, liability, spectrum, orbital debris, data governance and access to test facilities alongside market growth. Private participation needs predictable authorisation and reliable public infrastructure.|For AP, connect Sriharikota and supplier/skills opportunities without assigning every new national mission to a completed local facility. Track qualified systems, safe launches and service uptake rather than only MoUs or startup registrations.','An authorisation, a startup registration and a successful orbital mission are three different achievements.','What institutions are needed for a competitive and responsible Indian space economy?','DRISHTI',p='P2')
add('Science & Technology','04 · Engineering and materials','Rotating detonation engines: a demonstration is not deployment','The August magazine reports a startup demonstration of a rotating detonation engine. Its exact performance and first-in-India claim were not independently verified from test data, so the technological principle is the study focus.','Detonation is a supersonic combustion wave coupled with a shock, unlike ordinary subsonic deflagration.|A rotating detonation design sustains combustion waves travelling around an annular chamber; potential efficiency benefits motivate research.|Stable operation, thermal loads, materials durability and integration remain engineering challenges. A laboratory or ground demonstration is not a flight-qualified engine.','Thermodynamic potential and realised system performance differ. Propulsion must be evaluated across thrust, fuel efficiency, stability, weight and reliability.','Claims of breakthrough efficiency need transparent baselines and reproducible testing under relevant conditions. An engine may succeed at one operating point yet fail durability or controllability requirements.|For UPSC, explain the principle without assuming all rockets or aircraft will immediately adopt it. Public support should assess safety, intellectual capability and verified milestones rather than an unexamined national-first headline.','Supersonic combustion does not mean the demonstrated platform itself has completed hypersonic flight.','How should emerging propulsion technologies be evaluated from experiment to operational capability?','DRISHTI',p='P2')
add('Science & Technology','03 · Space capability and astronomy','August solar-eclipse revision: geometry determines totality','The August compilation discusses the 12 August 2026 total solar eclipse. This note consolidates eclipse mechanics; exact local visibility and timings require an authoritative location-specific ephemeris not inspected here.','A solar eclipse occurs when the Moon lies between Earth and Sun near new moon with suitable orbital alignment.|Observers within the Moon’s umbra can experience totality; penumbral observers see a partial eclipse, while an annular eclipse involves the antumbral region.|The Moon’s orbital inclination explains why an eclipse does not occur at every new moon. Apparent angular size helps determine total versus annular appearance.','Syzygy means approximate alignment of celestial bodies. Solar and lunar eclipses involve different alignments and shadow geometries.','Eclipses enable studies of the solar corona, but totality is geographically limited and brief. A global eclipse date is not proof that it is visible from AP or anywhere else in India.|Public communication should provide location-specific timings and safe viewing instructions. Looking at the partly eclipsed Sun requires appropriate certified solar protection; ordinary sunglasses are not a safe substitute.','A solar eclipse requires new moon, not full moon; a total eclipse somewhere is not total everywhere.','Explain how orbital geometry determines eclipse type and visibility.','DRISHTI',12,p='P2')
add('Science & Technology','05 · Scientific recognition','Deepak Dhar and the 2026 Dirac Medal: complexity from simple rules','ICTP’s primary announcement names four recipients for contributions extending statistical mechanics into complex systems, optimisation, neuroscience and AI. It confirms Deepak Dhar’s sandpile-model contribution.','The recipients are Bernard Derrida, Deepak Dhar, Marc Mézard and Haim Sompolinsky.|Dhar’s work explains how local interactions can generate avalanches of very different sizes and a spontaneously critical regime.|The Dirac Medal is awarded by ICTP in Trieste for theoretical-physics contributions; the announcement describes it as an award established in 1985.','Statistical mechanics connects microscopic rules to collective behaviour. Self-organised criticality describes systems that can approach critical regimes without ordinary fine-tuning of an external control parameter.','Applications to earthquakes, traffic or markets are models of collective behaviour, not guarantees of predicting the exact next event. Scientific recognition can be used to explain ideas rather than memorising biographies alone.|For exam recall, connect recipient, institution and contribution accurately. Do not confuse the ICTP medal with a Nobel Prize or imply that one simplified sandpile model fully describes every real complex system.','The 2026 medal has four co-recipients; it is not awarded solely to Dhar or for discovering a new fundamental particle.','How can statistical mechanics illuminate collective phenomena beyond traditional physics?','ICTP,DRISHTI',8,p='P2')
add('Science & Technology','04 · Engineering and materials','Lab-grown diamonds: physical identity is not geological rarity','The August compilation reviews synthetic diamonds and India’s technology push. It overstates equivalence by suggesting the same rarity; that claim is explicitly corrected here.','Lab-grown and mined diamonds share diamond’s crystalline carbon structure, while their production histories and some diagnostic growth signatures differ.|High-pressure high-temperature methods and chemical vapour deposition are two major production routes.|Laboratory origin does not automatically guarantee low emissions, ethical labour or complete traceability; power sources and supply-chain practices still matter.','Diamond’s hardness, thermal conductivity and defect engineering support industrial and quantum-related applications as well as jewellery. It is distinct from a mere imitation gemstone.','Compare lifecycle impacts using energy mix, process efficiency and verified sourcing, not origin labels alone. Industrial capability depends on seeds, reactors, process control and specialised skills.|For APPSC, connect advanced materials to value addition without inventing a new state subsidy or factory. Market prices and natural scarcity can differ even where chemical structure and useful properties are similar.','A lab-grown diamond is real diamond, but it does not possess the same geological scarcity as a mined stone.','Assess the industrial potential and sustainability conditions of lab-grown diamonds.','DRISHTI',p='P2')
add('Culture & History','01 · Freedom struggle and historical evidence','Royal Indian Navy uprising: an Independence Day linkage','The August magazine revisits the 1946 naval uprising in the context of Independence Day. This is historical revision, not a new August 2026 mutiny or discovery.','The strike began on 18 February 1946 in Bombay and spread through naval establishments with civilian and worker support.|Racial discrimination, poor service conditions and wider anti-colonial sentiment shaped the mobilisation.|The uprising challenged British confidence in the loyalty of an important coercive institution, but independence had multiple political, economic and international causes.','Compare the INA trials, labour mobilisation, post-war discontent and constitutional negotiations without collapsing them into one event or single-cause explanation.','Historical analysis should examine participants’ demands, leadership responses, repression and limits as well as symbolism. Solidarity across communities is important but does not erase the period’s complex political tensions.|For Mains, locate the uprising in the final phase of the freedom struggle and distinguish direct participant evidence from later interpretation. Do not equate the Royal Indian Navy with the independent republic’s present command structure.','The uprising occurred in February 1946; August 2026 is a commemorative study context.','Assess the significance and limits of the 1946 naval uprising in the final phase of colonial rule.','DRISHTI',p='P2')
add('Culture & History','01 · Freedom struggle and historical evidence','Hoysala hero stone: epigraphy can revise a historical chronology','Selected magazine reporting describes an Old Kannada inscription found during temple restoration at Neeragunda, Karnataka. Authentication and the proposed chronology remain important qualifications.','The reported Veeragallu commemorates a soldier, Boppa, and associates his death with the period shortly after ruler Vishnuvardhana’s death.|The magazine presents a possible 1141 CE chronological implication; the inscription and calendar conversion were not independently examined, so this is not a certified new final death date.|Hero stones memorialise acts of sacrifice or defence and can supply evidence about patronage, warfare, society and belief as well as royal chronology.','Hoysala centres include Belur and Halebidu. Epigraphic readings require script, language, calendrical and contextual evaluation; one artefact should be compared with other evidence.','A discovery headline should lead to a research question, not immediate replacement of every textbook date. Provenance, conservation, expert reading and publication enable scholarly verification.|For APPSC, compare memorial-stone traditions and South Indian inscriptional sources without relocating this Karnataka find to Andhra Pradesh. Archaeological value extends beyond a ruler’s biography to local social history.','The reported chronological interpretation is conditional; a magazine summary is not the original authenticated epigraphic edition.','How do inscriptions complement and challenge literary historical narratives?','DRISHTI',p='P2')
add('Society & Welfare','05 · Care economy and nutrition','Caregiving ecosystem: professionalisation must include unpaid carers','The August compilation discusses NITI Aayog’s caregiving report and proposals for an organised care ecosystem. Original report statistics were not independently verified; recommendations are not new universal benefits.','Care demand includes older persons, disability, chronic illness, dementia, home care and palliative support.|Paid professional care and unpaid family care are distinct but connected; women often carry a disproportionate unpaid burden.|Training standards, accreditation, respite services and mobility partnerships are proposed tools, not proof that caregiving is already uniformly regulated across India.','Article 41 addresses public assistance within its constitutional formulation. National accounts generally exclude unpaid own-household care services from measured production, without implying that they lack social value.','Professionalisation should improve dignity, safety and wages rather than merely prepare low-paid workers for export. Support family carers with accessible information, rest, referral and appropriate social protection.|For AP, connect ageing, disability assessment and rural service access. Training counts alone do not establish quality or job security; measure care-recipient outcomes and caregiver burden while maintaining consent and safeguards.','All care is not unpaid, and absence from measured GDP does not mean absence of economic contribution.','Design an inclusive care ecosystem that recognises both professional and family caregivers.','DRISHTI')
add('Society & Welfare','05 · Care economy and nutrition','SOFI 2026 revision: hunger and diet affordability differ','The August magazine reviews the multi-agency UN food-security report. This note is report-indexed revision, not an independently verified August publication date or a primary-table statistical audit.','Undernourishment, moderate/severe food insecurity, malnutrition and inability to afford a healthy diet measure different dimensions.|The review highlights Africa’s large hunger burden alongside continuing Asian nutritional challenges; exact comparative totals are not independently certified here.|Dietary diversity and the prices of nutritious foods can remain problematic even where staple-grain availability improves.','SOFI is associated with FAO, IFAD, UNICEF, WFP and WHO. SDG 2 includes hunger and nutrition rather than only aggregate cereal production.','Targeting calories alone can neglect proteins, micronutrients and maternal/child nutrition. Strengthen diverse production, cold chains, accessible health services and social protection while addressing conflict and climate shocks.|For AP, connect PDS reliability, locally available nutritious food and school/childcare nutrition without turning a global report into a new state entitlement. State outcomes require state-specific household and health evidence.','Food insecurity is not identical to undernourishment; a proposed NFSA amendment is not an operative reduction in existing ration entitlements.','Why must food security move beyond cereal availability to affordable nutritious diets?','DRISHTI',p='P2')
add('Andhra Pradesh','01 · Infrastructure and fiscal governance','AP draft PPP policy: a bridge fund is not free infrastructure','PTI reports that the 6 August Cabinet approved a draft PPP policy with a ₹1,500 crore Viability Gap/Bridge Fund. The original policy notification and project contracts were not inspected.','The reported fund is intended to improve the viability of infrastructure partnerships; it is not evidence that ₹1,500 crore has already been spent.|A draft-policy approval differs from notification, project appraisal, tender award, financial closure and commercial operation.|PPP arrangements can create future payment or guarantee obligations even when construction is privately financed.','Viability-gap support addresses a gap between economic/social desirability and commercial returns. It should be distinguished from guarantees, equity participation and routine departmental spending.','Require published appraisal, realistic demand estimates and transparent procurement. Public support should purchase additional public value, not merely absorb risks a concessionaire could manage.|For AP, integrate affordability, land acquisition, service standards, independent monitoring and contingent-liability reporting. Count completed, performing assets rather than combining fund ceilings, project costs and private investment announcements into one expenditure total.','Cabinet approval of a draft PPP policy is not enactment of a PPP statute or completed spending.','What safeguards should accompany Andhra Pradesh’s expanded use of infrastructure PPPs?','APCAB',6,True)
add('Andhra Pradesh','02 · Law, safety and public institutions','Disha Bill withdrawal: do not call it repeal of an operative Act','PTI reports Cabinet approval to withdraw the Disha Bill. The precise legislative history and subsequent Assembly action require separate verification; the Cabinet decision alone is not completed repeal.','The reported measure concerns withdrawal of a Bill rather than a verified repeal of an already commenced state criminal statute.|Cabinet approval precedes or authorises further legislative action; it does not by itself prove that every parliamentary or assent stage has occurred.|The underlying withdrawal proceedings and final operative legal position were not independently inspected in this batch.','A Bill, an assented Act, a commenced provision and an executive programme are distinct. Women’s safety policy also depends on policing, investigation, prosecution and survivor support.','Avoid equating withdrawal of a proposed law with removal of all protection for women, or equating a fast-trial promise with reliable justice. Existing applicable criminal and procedural law must be analysed separately.|For APPSC, discuss forensic capacity, trained investigators, legal aid, witness safety and timely reasoned trials. Rights safeguards are part of effective justice rather than obstacles to be removed by a deadline.','Withdrawal of a Bill is not automatically repeal of an enacted and commenced law.','Why does women’s safety require institutional capacity beyond special-law announcements?','APCAB',6,True)
add('Andhra Pradesh','01 · Infrastructure and fiscal governance','Amaravati approvals: distinguish re-tendering, loans and compensation','The 6 August Cabinet report includes capital-region projects, financing ratification and land-related compensation. These are separate transaction categories, not one newly completed capital-city investment.','The reviewed decisions include re-tendering and twin-tower project measures linked to Amaravati development.|PTI reports ratification of a ₹300 crore APCRDA loan and ₹122.2 crore compensation associated with Vaikuntapuram.|A borrowing approval, loan ratification, compensation payment and construction expenditure should not be added without checking overlap, dates and actual disbursement.','Capital development involves planning, land rights, public finance, procurement and environmental compliance. A development authority’s debt may create broader public-sector fiscal exposure.','A good project dashboard should show tender status, contract value, work progress, financing source and service outcomes. Independent quality checks and accessible land-grievance mechanisms help protect public value.|For Mains, balance administrative-capital needs with long-term debt service, regional inclusion and resilient design. Avoid interpreting re-tendering as completion or assuming that a ratified loan is a fresh grant from the Union.','₹300 crore loan ratification is not the same as a new ₹300 crore grant or audited construction spending.','How can capital-city infrastructure be made financially transparent and socially accountable?','APCAB',6,True)
add('Andhra Pradesh','03 · Livelihoods and regional economy','Aquaculture power support: a tariff decision is not a loan waiver','PTI reports the Cabinet’s aquaculture electricity-support decision on 6 August, with an effective date stated as 1 June. August reporting and retrospective policy applicability must remain distinct.','The reviewed support sets an eligible aquaculture power tariff at ₹1.50 per unit under the reported decision.|The effective date stated in the report is 1 June, not the Cabinet reporting date.|Eligibility conditions, distribution-company billing adjustments and the original GO were not independently verified; the note does not promise identical benefits to every pond or consumer category.','An electricity tariff subsidy reduces an eligible input cost; it is different from a cash grant, farm-debt waiver or cancellation of all electricity arrears.','For AP’s aqua economy, assess cost relief alongside water quality, disease risk, export demand, energy efficiency and grid reliability. A cheap input can improve competitiveness but also encourage unsustainable resource use if other constraints are ignored.|Evaluate fiscal incidence and actual billed support transparently. Benefits should be measured through eligible receipts and viable livelihoods, not only the headline tariff or a projected aggregate subsidy.','The report’s June effective date does not make this a new June discovery; it is an August-reviewed Cabinet decision.','Evaluate input subsidies as a tool for sustainable aquaculture competitiveness.','APCAB',6,True)
add('Andhra Pradesh','04 · Public spaces, culture and tourism','Godavari Pushkaralu: August ghat approvals are preparation, not event completion','The August Cabinet report reviews preparations for the 2027 Godavari Pushkaralu, including ghat works. Do not confuse the planning year with the event year or add overlapping July approvals.','PTI reports ₹210.06 crore for 238 ghats across six districts.|The works relate to the 2027 event; approval in August 2026 does not establish that the event occurred or all works were completed.|Earlier July first-phase figures may overlap or concern different packages; original sanction orders and package reconciliation were not inspected.','Mass-gathering management combines cultural access with crowd safety, water quality, public health, transport and temporary-service design.','Use package-wise accounting, site readiness and independent safety certification rather than a single spending total. Design inclusive access, evacuation routes, sanitation, drinking water and safe riverfront barriers.|For APPSC, connect pilgrimage infrastructure to durable local utility without assuming every temporary asset becomes a long-term development gain. Track actual readiness, environmental compliance and maintenance responsibilities.','₹210.06 crore is a reported approved package, not independently verified utilisation or an amount safely added to every July headline.','How should mass-gathering infrastructure balance cultural participation, safety and fiscal accountability?','APCAB',6,True,p='P2')
add('Andhra Pradesh','05 · Education and urban institutions','Education and urban approvals: capacity is more than a new institution','The 6 August Cabinet report includes education posts, brownfield university proposals, urban-authority measures and financing. This combined institutional note avoids treating each approval as a functioning new service.','PTI reports approval of 15 District Educational Officer posts and brownfield-university measures.|Urban development authority decisions and a reported ₹680.2 crore HUDCO financing item for APBDICL are part of the same Cabinet account.|The original orders, recruitment completion, final university authorisations and loan-use records were not independently inspected.','A sanctioned post differs from a filled post; institutional approval differs from academic accreditation and operational capacity. Urban authorities also require clear mandates and accounts.','For education, connect administrative staffing to supervision, school quality and accessible grievance resolution. For urban development, align institutional boundaries with planning, utilities and financial responsibility.|Avoid aggregating a loan amount, post count and university proposal into a generic development-success claim. APPSC answers should identify the responsible institution, necessary next step and observable service outcome for each decision.','15 approved posts do not mean 15 newly appointed officers; a loan is not a budget grant.','Why do institutional approvals require staffing, finance and accountability to become effective services?','APCAB',6,True,p='P2')
add('Andhra Pradesh','02 · Law, safety and public institutions','Pedestrian safety: design streets for people, not only vehicle flow','PTI’s Cabinet account includes a pedestrian-safety measure. The final policy text, road-design standards and implementation orders were not independently read, so this note separates the approval from original analytical guidance.','The reported Cabinet decision places pedestrian safety within state policy priorities.|Approval does not establish that all city streets have accessible footpaths, safe crossings or enforced speed management.|No verified district-wise project cost, casualty reduction or completed rollout is claimed from the short report.','Road safety follows a safe-system approach: road users can make mistakes, so infrastructure, speed, vehicles and emergency care should limit fatal consequences.','For AP towns, continuous footpaths, universal accessibility, shade, drainage, safe school access and crossings at desire lines can improve both safety and inclusion. These are analytical recommendations, not claims about the unseen policy’s exact clauses.|Use exposure-sensitive monitoring, audits and maintenance responsibility rather than counting painted markings alone. Protect informal workers and transit users through consultative design; safety should not become indiscriminate displacement of vulnerable street users.','A pedestrian-policy approval does not prove completed street redesign or a measured fall in deaths.','How can Andhra Pradesh integrate pedestrian safety with inclusive urban mobility?','APCAB',6,True,p='P2')
add('Andhra Pradesh','03 · Livelihoods and regional economy','Netanna Sevalo: distinguish launch, reported release and verified receipt','The New Indian Express reports the scheme’s 7 August launch at Kothapeta in Chirala constituency, Bapatla district, during National Handloom Day celebrations. The primary GO and bank-credit audit were not inspected.','The report states ₹179 crore released for 71,536 eligible handloom-weaver families, at ₹25,000 per beneficiary family.|The amount is a rounded aggregate: 71,536 × ₹25,000 = ₹178.84 crore, consistent with a rounded ₹179 crore headline.|A reported release is not independent verification that every intended household’s account was credited or that all potentially eligible families were included.','Direct benefit support can cushion working-capital or livelihood stress. Eligibility design, grievance correction and reliable payment systems determine distributive effectiveness.','For APPSC, link welfare to the wider handloom ecosystem: yarn, design, cooperative credit, authenticity, marketing and demand. Cash assistance alone cannot guarantee competitive products or sustainable employment.|Compare family count, per-family benefit and actual receipt separately. Do not convert a launch report into a universal entitlement for all textile employees or a promise of benefits under unread future guidelines.','₹179 crore is rounded; the scheme concerns eligible handloom families, not every large textile firm.','How can cash support complement a sustainable revival of Andhra Pradesh’s handloom economy?','APWEAVE',7,True)
add('Andhra Pradesh','03 · Livelihoods and regional economy','Handloom electricity support: differentiate families and powerloom units','The Chirala programme report describes existing electricity support alongside the Netanna launch. This is August-reviewed scheme context, not independently verified introduction of a completely new power policy that day.','The report describes up to 200 free units per month for eligible handloom-weaver families and 500 units for powerloom units.|It reports 62,541 handloom families and 10,032 powerloom units under this support, distinct from the 71,536 Netanna beneficiary-family count.|The exact GO, metering conditions and implementation audit were not independently inspected; counts from different schemes must not be treated as the same denominator.','A household/family, a loom, a production unit and a registered beneficiary are different statistical units. Traditional handloom and mechanised powerloom are not identical production categories.','Assess whether power relief reduces genuine production costs while targeting and metering remain transparent. Combine support with reliable supply and energy-efficient equipment, avoiding incentives for misclassification.|For AP, compare input relief with design and market interventions rather than assuming all weavers face the same costs. A difference between scheme counts is not itself proof of fraud or exclusion without eligibility reconciliation.','200 and 500 units apply to different reported categories, not two additive entitlements for every weaver.','What indicators would show whether handloom input support improves viable livelihoods?','APWEAVE',7,True,p='P2')
add('Andhra Pradesh','02 · Law, safety and public institutions','BC protection and health insurance announcements: wait for operative terms','The 8 August newspaper report records the Chief Minister’s statements on a prepared BC Protection Bill and universal health insurance. These political/executive announcements are not verified final legal entitlements.','The report says a draft BC Protection Bill had been prepared and legislation was intended; it does not establish completed passage or commencement.|A ₹2.5 lakh universal-health-insurance announcement appears in the same account.|The insurance package, enrolment rules, insurer arrangements, start date and actual claims were not independently inspected; no unconditional benefit promise is made here.','Legislation, executive programme design and insurance contracts operate through different instruments. Coverage ceilings differ from cash transfers and actual out-of-pocket protection.','For BC empowerment, evaluate accessible remedies, institutional capacity and lawful safeguards rather than only a proposed title. For health, examine covered services, exclusions, provider access, claim denials and continuity of care.|Maintain a policy-stage ledger before writing APPSC answers. A proposal can be important current affairs without being misrepresented as an operational right; later verified orders should be linked as separate developments.','“On the anvil” is not “in force”; ₹2.5 lakh announced coverage is not automatic cash to every resident.','Why should policy evaluation distinguish announcements from enforceable and accessible benefits?','APWEAVE',7,True,p='P2')
add('Andhra Pradesh','04 · Public spaces, culture and tourism','Chirala coastal tourism: link the corridor to local producers','The Chirala handloom programme report describes a ₹97 crore Suryalanka–Chirala tourism corridor under Swadesh Darshan, a Bay of Bapatla website and the LEAPS skills initiative. Completion was not independently verified.','The coastal belt discussed includes Suryalanka, Bapatla and Chirala.|The report describes the corridor as being developed, not a fully completed and audited visitor infrastructure system.|The website, skills programme and agreements with organisations including Fabindia are distinct interventions; an agreement is not proof of realised sales or permanent jobs.','Destination development combines public infrastructure, local enterprises, environmental carrying capacity and accessible visitor information. A tourism corridor is not merely a road.','For AP, connect beach access and safety to local weaving, food, guiding and homestays. Preserve coastal ecology, waste systems and disaster readiness rather than maximising visitor counts regardless of impact.|Measure local retention of tourism income, skilled employment, product authenticity and community participation. Digital promotion cannot substitute for safe infrastructure, reliable information or transparent procurement.','₹97 crore is a reported development amount, not verified completed expenditure or guaranteed revenue for every artisan.','How can coastal tourism strengthen local handloom livelihoods without undermining coastal resilience?','APWEAVE',7,True,p='P2')
add('Andhra Pradesh','06 · Wildlife and landscape coexistence','Visakhapatnam elephant programme: the ₹500 crore plan is regional','PIB’s release posted on 13 August describes national elephant-conservation celebrations, a southern regional action plan and the Project Elephant steering meeting. Because its text says “today” while the annual day is 12 August, the note uses the reporting date.','The regional plan covers Karnataka, Kerala, Andhra Pradesh and Tamil Nadu with ₹500 crore support from the Union; this is not an AP-only allocation.|Its focus includes habitat connectivity, corridors, early warning, conflict mitigation, restoration, community participation and interstate coordination.|The release also describes the 23rd Project Elephant steering meeting and conservation publications. Project Elephant began in 1992 as a Centrally Sponsored Scheme.','Asian elephant conservation requires connected landscapes across administrative borders. A corridor’s ecological function differs from a state boundary or a fenced reserve.','For AP, combine land-use planning, crop protection, safe power infrastructure, rapid response and timely assistance with community participation. A plan launch does not establish that all corridors are protected or conflict deaths have fallen.|Use ground-validated monitoring and cooperative protocols with neighbouring states. Forest departments, railways, electricity agencies and local communities need shared responsibilities; technology alone cannot restore blocked habitat connectivity.','₹500 crore covers the four-state regional plan, not a verified ₹500 crore exclusively for Andhra Pradesh.','Design an interstate human–elephant coexistence strategy relevant to Andhra Pradesh.','ELEPHANT,DRISHTI',13,True,precision='reporting-day')
add('Andhra Pradesh','06 · Wildlife and landscape coexistence','HANUMAN and conflict mitigation: technology needs field capacity','The same PIB conservation release describes Andhra Pradesh’s HANUMAN initiative and its “conflict to coexistence” approach. It reports the initiative, not an independently audited statewide service rollout.','HANUMAN expands as Healing And Nurturing Units for Monitoring, Aid and Nursing of Wildlife.|PIB describes modern technology, local-community engagement and frontline-staff capacity building in the state’s wildlife approach.|The release’s broader workshop addresses veterinary interventions, electrocution, railway mortality, habitats and corridors; these are not evidence that every proposed intervention is already installed in AP.','Wildlife rescue, veterinary care, early warning and habitat conservation are complementary but different functions. A rescued animal count is not a complete conservation outcome.','For APPSC, propose clear response protocols, trained staff, safe equipment and coordination with health/power/rail agencies. Claims of AI detection need validation of false alarms, missed animals and maintenance needs.|Community trust depends on timely response, transparent compensation and accessible reporting, not only apps or acronyms. Evaluate human safety and animal welfare together while avoiding dangerous amateur rescue or unscientific translocation.','An initiative’s expansion and stated goals do not establish the number of operational units or a measured fall in conflict.','How can field institutions convert wildlife-monitoring technology into safer coexistence?','ELEPHANT',13,True,p='P2',precision='reporting-day')
add('Andhra Pradesh','07 · Digital infrastructure and environmental governance','Visakhapatnam data-centre debate: investment needs a resource budget','Selected August magazine passages discuss sustainability concerns around proposed mega data centres in the Visakhapatnam–Anakapalli region. This is a thematic local-policy debate, not a new independently verified August investment sanction.','Data centres combine servers, power, cooling, networks and security to provide computing and storage services.|The magazine references a proposed 1 GW hub; proposed electrical scale is not the same as measured annual electricity consumption, water demand or completed operational capacity.|Original project clearances, cooling design, resource-use assessments and protest accounts were not independently inspected here; no precise state water-allocation claim is made.','Power is measured in watts; energy in watt-hours. IT load, total facility load and grid connection capacity differ; PUE and water-use metrics require carefully defined boundaries.','For AP, require transparent power sourcing, water balance, cooling choice, drought resilience, wastewater reuse and local infrastructure cost allocation. Renewable procurement does not eliminate every grid or water constraint.|Evaluate employment quality, domestic digital capability and public benefits against resource and opportunity costs. An investment announcement cannot alone establish net social value, nor does a resource concern automatically prove a project is unlawful.','1 GW is a power scale, not 1 GWh of annual consumption or proof that the facility is already running.','How can Andhra Pradesh attract digital infrastructure while safeguarding water and energy security?','DRISHTI',ap=True,p='P2')
